  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/7fe5dad64f7c065e693ed407e0f291ec995b4823a0356e11055d20a0ca89a53b.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =16\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(2) & =\\frac{16\\pi }{2}+\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi +4\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
